/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Glucocorticoid receptors \(GRs\), encoded by the NR3C1 gene, are integral members of the nuclear receptor superfamily, functioning as ligand-activated transcription factors that mediate cellular responses to glucocorticoid hormones, such as cortisol. These receptors play a pivotal role in regulating gene expression by interacting with glucocorticoid response elements \(GREs\) within DNA, thereby modulating a diverse array of physiological processes, including cellular proliferation, survival, and stress responses [1]. In the context of oncology, aberrant GR expression has been implicated in the progression of various malignancies, notably breast and prostate cancers, by promoting tumor growth, metastasis, and resistance to therapeutic interventions. Structurally, GR is a modular protein comprising approximately 777 amino acids, with a molecular weight of approximately 85.7 kDa [2]. It consists of three primary functional domains: the N- terminal domain \(NTD\), the DNA-binding domain \(DBD\), and the ligand-binding domain \(LBD\). The NTD facilitates transcriptional activation by recruiting co-activator proteins, while the DBD, a highly conserved region, binds specifically to GREs to regulate gene expression. The LBD is critical for hormone binding, enabling GR to interact with glucocorticoids and orchestrate both gene activation and repression through interactions with co-regulatory proteins [3]. A hinge region connects the DBD and LBD, conferring structural flexibility, while a C-terminal extension further modulates receptor activity. Overexpression of GR has been associated with enhanced activation of oncogenic signaling pathways, notably the mitogen-activated protein kinase \(MAPK\) and phosphoinositide 3-kinase \(PI3K\)/AKT pathways, which are implicated in cancer progression [4]. These pathways promote epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition \(EMT\), a critical process in metastasis, and enhance tumor cell survival under therapeutic stress, such as chemotherapy. Furthermore, GR may interact with other signaling molecules, such as the epidermal growth factor receptor \(EGFR\), amplifying proliferative signals and contributing to tumor aggressiveness. In breast cancer, GR expression is observed in approximately 60% of luminal breast cancers \(subtypes A and B\), where its dysregulation is associated with increased tumor invasiveness and resistance to endocrine therapies [1]. The molecular mechanisms underlying GR-mediated oncogenesis involve complex interactions between GR and its downstream targets. Dysregulation, particularly in the ligand-binding domain, may result in altered receptor activity, further exacerbating )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
