888 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (techniques that can predict drug-target interactions and identify potential drug candidates for further testing.  Artificial neural networks \(ANNs\) are a category of machine learning algorithms applied  in glucocorticoid receptor \(GR\) research. Protein-protein docking has been used to study  interactions between wild-type GR and its co-regulatory proteins, alongside complexes  influenced by specific mutations. ANN-based analyses have assessed how mutations  affect GR interactions, with findings indicating that the A207G mutation significantly  disrupts these interactions \(Patel et al., 2020\). A major limitation of ANNs is their  requirement for extensive datasets for effective training, posing challenges in studies  with limited data availability.  Virtual screening via molecular docking has been instrumental in evaluating the binding  affinity of potential GR-targeted drug candidates. Research has employed three- dimensional structure-based pharmacophore modeling to explore the structural and  chemical interactions of compounds like dexamethasone with the GR \(Kumar et al.,  2018\). In silico screening approaches have identified novel GR modulators, with  compounds such as quercetin and apigenin showing promising anti-inflammatory  properties, indicating potential for therapeutic applications \(Singh et al., 2021\).  Molecular docking simulations have also investigated new drug combinations, such as  fluticasone and budesonide, for their interactions with GR-related proteins,  demonstrating improved binding affinities compared to traditional treatments like  cortisol \(Gupta et al., 2021\). In 2022, studies screened publicly available databases to identify inhibitors of the ABCG2  protein, a member of the ABC transporter family associated with drug resistance in GR- related pathways \(Rahman et al., 2022\). In silico techniques have also identified halogen- based derivatives as potent GR inhibitors, with molecular dynamics simulations  elucidating their stability and efficacy \(Khan et al., 2022\). Another study utilized  molecular docking to identify seven promising drug candidates for addressing drug- resistant conditions linked to GR dysregulation \(Ali et al., 2022\). These computational  approaches underscore their value in advancing the discovery of novel GR-targeted  therapies. )
/E (techniques that can predict drug-target interactions and identify potential drug candidates for further testing.  Artificial neural networks \(ANNs\) are a category of machine learning algorithms applied  in glucocorticoid receptor \(GR\) research. Protein-protein docking has been used to study  interactions between wild-type GR and its co-regulatory proteins, alongside complexes  influenced by specific mutations. ANN-based analyses have assessed how mutations  affect GR interactions, with findings indicating that the A207G mutation significantly  disrupts these interactions \(Patel et al., 2020\). A major limitation of ANNs is their  requirement for extensive datasets for effective training, posing challenges in studies  with limited data availability.  Virtual screening via molecular docking has been instrumental in evaluating the binding  affinity of potential GR-targeted drug candidates. Research has employed three- dimensional structure-based pharmacophore modeling to explore the structural and  chemical interactions of compounds like dexamethasone with the GR \(Kumar et al.,  2018\). In silico screening approaches have identified novel GR modulators, with  compounds such as quercetin and apigenin showing promising anti-inflammatory  properties, indicating potential for therapeutic applications \(Singh et al., 2021\).  Molecular docking simulations have also investigated new drug combinations, such as  fluticasone and budesonide, for their interactions with GR-related proteins,  demonstrating improved binding affinities compared to traditional treatments like  cortisol \(Gupta et al., 2021\). In 2022, studies screened publicly available databases to identify inhibitors of the ABCG2  protein, a member of the ABC transporter family associated with drug resistance in GR- related pathways \(Rahman et al., 2022\). In silico techniques have also identified halogen- based derivatives as potent GR inhibitors, with molecular dynamics simulations  elucidating their stability and efficacy \(Khan et al., 2022\). Another study utilized  molecular docking to identify seven promising drug candidates for addressing drug- resistant conditions linked to GR dysregulation \(Ali et al., 2022\). These computational  approaches underscore their value in advancing the discovery of novel GR-targeted  therapies. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
