907 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (of phytochemicals derived from Curcuma longa against GR, highlighting their potential as anti-inflammatory agents \(Singh et al., 2022\).  QSAR models screen large databases and increase the chances of discovering highly  potent drug candidates in a relatively shorter period. These models have been shown to  provide accurate predictions of the biological activities of compounds and result in  reduced pre-clinical testing, thereby presenting a cost-effective alternative option in drug  discovery. This technique can also be used to recognize the key molecular and chemical  characteristics in a compound that impact its biological activity \(Bauer et al., 2017;  Hansch et al., 1996\). By determining the compounds with the highest predicted activity  or lowest predicted toxicity, QSAR modeling can help prioritize which molecules should  be synthesized and tested in the lab. Such information can be further utilized in designing  new drugs with higher efficacy and selectivity.  However, QSAR models are only as good as the data used to train them, both in terms of  quality and quantity. If the training data is skewed or limited, the model may fail to  predict the properties of new molecules accurately \(Dunn & Hansch, 1974\). These models  are also affected by the selected molecular descriptors that represent the molecules in  the dataset. Also, the statistical methods used to build or validate a QSAR model impact  the results \(Gao et al., 1998\). While QSAR modeling is a good and dependable screening  tool, it cannot act as a replacement for experimental validation. Pharmacoinformatic techniques have become increasingly important in the discovery  and development of drugs for a variety of diseases, including breast cancer. We  investigated pharmacoinformatic methodologies like quantitative structure-activity  relationship \(QSAR\) modelling that can aid in GR agonist analysis. Furthermore, we  explored the current state of pharmacoinformatic approaches, their limitations, and  future research directions in this field. By utilizing advanced computational tools and  techniques, pharmacoinformatic has the potential to make significant contributions to  the development of novel and effective drugs for treatment. )
/E (of phytochemicals derived from Curcuma longa against GR, highlighting their potential as anti-inflammatory agents \(Singh et al., 2022\).  QSAR models screen large databases and increase the chances of discovering highly  potent drug candidates in a relatively shorter period. These models have been shown to  provide accurate predictions of the biological activities of compounds and result in  reduced pre-clinical testing, thereby presenting a cost-effective alternative option in drug  discovery. This technique can also be used to recognize the key molecular and chemical  characteristics in a compound that impact its biological activity \(Bauer et al., 2017;  Hansch et al., 1996\). By determining the compounds with the highest predicted activity  or lowest predicted toxicity, QSAR modeling can help prioritize which molecules should  be synthesized and tested in the lab. Such information can be further utilized in designing  new drugs with higher efficacy and selectivity.  However, QSAR models are only as good as the data used to train them, both in terms of  quality and quantity. If the training data is skewed or limited, the model may fail to  predict the properties of new molecules accurately \(Dunn & Hansch, 1974\). These models  are also affected by the selected molecular descriptors that represent the molecules in  the dataset. Also, the statistical methods used to build or validate a QSAR model impact  the results \(Gao et al., 1998\). While QSAR modeling is a good and dependable screening  tool, it cannot act as a replacement for experimental validation. Pharmacoinformatic techniques have become increasingly important in the discovery  and development of drugs for a variety of diseases, including breast cancer. We  investigated pharmacoinformatic methodologies like quantitative structure-activity  relationship \(QSAR\) modelling that can aid in GR agonist analysis. Furthermore, we  explored the current state of pharmacoinformatic approaches, their limitations, and  future research directions in this field. By utilizing advanced computational tools and  techniques, pharmacoinformatic has the potential to make significant contributions to  the development of novel and effective drugs for treatment. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
